<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  14.4 The Chain Rule
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-chain-rule" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Chain Rule</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
State and apply the chain rule for a function <m>w = f(x,y)</m> whose
intermediate variables <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are functions of a single
independent variable <m>t</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Extend the chain rule to three intermediate variables, and to two
independent variables <m>r</m> and <m>s</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Draw a tree diagram for a composition and read the corresponding chain
rule formula off the diagram.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Use the chain rule in applied settings, for instance to find the rate of
change of temperature experienced by an object moving along a path.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
The chain rule for functions of one variable tells us how to
differentiate a composition. When a function of several variables has
inputs that themselves depend on other variables, there is a
corresponding rule, and it has one term for each intermediate variable.
Tree diagrams give a reliable way of writing these formulas down without
memorizing them.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cr-one-independent">
<title>One Independent Variable</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-chain-rule-two-intermediate">
<title>Chain Rule for Functions of Two Independent Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two-variable function <m>w = f(x,y)</m>. If the following
conditions are satisfied:
</p>
<p>
<alert>1.</alert> <m>w = f(x,y)</m> has continuous partial derivatives
<m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m>,
</p>
<p>
<alert>2.</alert> <m>x = x(t)</m> and <m>y = y(t)</m> are differentiable
functions of <m>t</m>,
</p>
<p>
then the function <m>w = f(x(t), y(t))</m> is a differentiable function
of <m>t</m> and
<md xml:id="eq-chain-rule-two" number="yes">
\frac{dw}{dt}
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-cr-tree-two"/> provides an easy way to remember the chain
rule. Note that <m>w</m> is the <term>dependent variable</term> since it
depends on <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, and <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are known as
the <term>intermediate variables</term>, whereas <m>t</m> is the
<term>independent variable</term>. To use the diagram, read every path
from <m>w</m> down to <m>t</m>, multiply the derivatives along each path,
and add the results.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-tree-two">
<caption>
Tree diagram for <m>w = f(x(t), y(t))</m>. There are two paths from
<m>w</m> down to <m>t</m>, one through <m>x</m> and one through <m>y</m>,
and the chain rule adds one term for each.
</caption>
<image width="46%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-tree-two">
<diagram dimensions="(280,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-0.5,1.7,3.5)">
<line at="t2-wx" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.2,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t2-wy" endpoints="((0,3),(1.2,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t2-xt" endpoints="((-1.2,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t2-yt" endpoints="((1.2,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="t2-w" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="t2-x" p="(-1.2,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t2-y" p="(1.2,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t2-t" p="(0,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="t2-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n">
<m>w=f(x,y)</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-x-lab" anchor="(-1.3,1.5)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>x</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-y-lab" anchor="(1.3,1.5)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>y</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-t-lab" anchor="(0,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
<m>t</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-wx-lab" anchor="(-0.72,2.4)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-wy-lab" anchor="(0.72,2.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-xt-lab" anchor="(-0.72,0.6)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="t2-yt-lab" anchor="(0.72,0.6)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A diamond shaped tree diagram. At the top is w equals f of x y. Two dashed blue branches descend from it, the left labelled partial f partial x leading to a node x, and the right labelled partial f partial y leading to a node y. From x and y two further branches, labelled d x d t and d y d t, descend and meet at the bottom node t.">
  <annotation ref="t2-w" text="The dependent variable w at the top of the tree."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-x" text="The intermediate variable x."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-y" text="The intermediate variable y."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-t" text="The independent variable t at the bottom."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-wx" text="The branch from w to x contributes the factor partial f partial x."/>
  <annotation ref="t2-xt" text="The branch from x to t contributes the factor d x d t. Multiplying along this path gives the first term of the chain rule."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<!-- Geometric intuition for the chain rule and the hiker example: from the
     instructor's handout "Walking uphill: why do the rates add?" (Nov 2026).
     The contour picture is schematic, not a measured map. -->
<p>
We now give a <term>geometric intuition</term> behind the chain rule
<xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/>: why it holds, and in particular why the
two rates <em>add</em>, without ever finding an equation for the surface.
Imagine a hiker walking over terrain whose elevation is
<m>w = f(x,y)</m>, where <m>x</m> measures eastward position and
<m>y</m> measures northward position. Both coordinates change with time,
<m>x = x(t)</m> and <m>y = y(t)</m>, and we want to know <em>how quickly
the hiker's elevation changes</em>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-hiker-split">
<caption>
One movement, two components (a top view with schematic contours). The
hiker may walk directly from <m>A</m> to <m>C</m>; we imagine going from
<m>A</m> to <m>B</m> and then from <m>B</m> to <m>C</m> only to separate
the two contributions to the change in elevation.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-hiker-split">
<diagram dimensions="(520,260)" margins="6">
<coordinates bbox="(0,0,10,5)">
<rectangle at="hs-ground" lower-left="(-1,-1)" dimensions="(12,7)" fill="#eef2f5" stroke="none"/>
<group at="hs-contours">
<circle center="(12.5,7.5)" radius="9.5" fill="#e3eee6" stroke="#9cbfa6" thickness="1"/>
<circle center="(12.5,7.5)" radius="7.5" fill="#cfe3d5" stroke="#9cbfa6" thickness="1"/>
<circle center="(12.5,7.5)" radius="5.5" fill="#b5d4bf" stroke="#9cbfa6" thickness="1"/>
<circle center="(12.5,7.5)" radius="3.5" fill="#94c0a3" stroke="#7fa98c" thickness="1"/>
</group>
<label at="hs-higher" anchor="(9.85,4.85)" alignment="sw"><m>\text{higher ground}</m></label>
<group at="hs-compass">
<line endpoints="((0.45,3.55),(1.35,3.55))" stroke="#444444" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
<line endpoints="((0.45,3.55),(0.45,4.55))" stroke="#444444" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
<label anchor="(1.35,3.55)" alignment="e" offset="(2,0)"><m>\text{east}</m></label>
<label anchor="(0.45,4.55)" alignment="e" offset="(4,-2)"><m>\text{north}</m></label>
</group>
<line at="hs-diagonal" endpoints="((1.5,1.2),(6.5,3.7))" stroke="#555555" dash="6 4" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
<line at="hs-east" endpoints="((1.5,1.2),(6.5,1.2))" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<line at="hs-north" endpoints="((6.5,1.2),(6.5,3.7))" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<point at="hs-A" p="(1.5,1.2)" fill="white" size="4"/>
<point at="hs-B" p="(6.5,1.2)" fill="white" size="4"/>
<point at="hs-C" p="(6.5,3.7)" fill="white" size="4"/>
<label anchor="(1.5,1.2)" alignment="sw" offset="(-2,-2)"><m>A</m></label>
<label anchor="(6.5,1.2)" alignment="se" offset="(2,-2)"><m>B</m></label>
<label anchor="(6.5,3.7)" alignment="nw" offset="(-2,2)"><m>C</m></label>
<label anchor="(4,1.2)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-4)" color="#0072B2"><m>\text{east: } \Delta x</m></label>
<label anchor="(6.5,2.45)" alignment="e" offset="(6,0)" color="#D55E00"><m>\text{north: } \Delta y</m></label>
<label anchor="(3.5,2.2)" alignment="nw" offset="(-2,2)"><m>\text{actual diagonal motion}</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A top view of the hiker's terrain. Shaded contour bands in the upper right mark higher ground. The hiker's actual motion is a dashed diagonal arrow from point A to point C. It is split into a blue arrow due east from A to B, labelled east delta x, followed by an orange arrow due north from B to C, labelled north delta y.">
  <annotation ref="hs-contours" text="Schematic contour bands; the ground rises toward the upper right corner."/>
  <annotation ref="hs-diagonal" text="The actual diagonal motion from A to C, drawn dashed."/>
  <annotation ref="hs-east" text="The eastward stage, from A to B, a displacement delta x. It contributes about f x times delta x to the change in elevation." sonify="no"/>
  <annotation ref="hs-north" text="The northward stage, from B to C, a displacement delta y. It contributes about f y times delta y to the change in elevation." sonify="no"/>
  <annotation ref="hs-compass" text="Compass arrows: east points right and north points up."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>Why splitting the motion works.</alert> The total change in
elevation depends only on the <em>starting and ending positions</em>, not
on which route we imagine between them, so we may compute it in two
stages:
<ol>
<li>
<p>
<alert>Move east.</alert> Over a very small eastward displacement
<m>\Delta x</m>, the local eastward slope <m>f_x</m> gives an elevation
change of approximately <m>f_x\,\Delta x</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<alert>Move north.</alert> Over a very small northward displacement
<m>\Delta y</m>, the local northward slope <m>f_y</m> gives an additional
elevation change of approximately <m>f_y\,\Delta y</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
Together,
<md>
\underbrace{\Delta w}_{\text{total}}
\approx \underbrace{f_x\,\Delta x}_{\text{east}}
+ \underbrace{f_y\,\Delta y}_{\text{north}} .
</md>
For a tiny displacement the slopes vary very little. (The northward slope
is really taken at <m>B</m> rather than at <m>A</m>; the continuity of
<m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> in <xref ref="thm-chain-rule-two-intermediate"/>
is what makes that difference negligible.) Dividing by the elapsed time
<m>\Delta t</m>,
<md>
\frac{\Delta w}{\Delta t}
\approx f_x\,\frac{\Delta x}{\Delta t} + f_y\,\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta t},
</md>
and letting <m>\Delta t \to 0</m> gives
<md>
\frac{dw}{dt}
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt},
</md>
which is <xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>The central idea:</alert> each coordinate motion contributes to the
rate of climbing, and those contributions <em>add</em>. A negative
contribution means that component of the motion carries the hiker
downhill.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-cr-hiker">
<title>How Fast Is the Hiker Climbing?</title>
<statement>
<p>
At a particular instant, a hiker is walking on a hill whose elevation (in
meters) is <m>w = f(x,y)</m>, with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> measured in
kilometers east and north, respectively. We do not know a formula for the
hill, only its slopes at the hiker's current position,
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = 40\,\frac{\text{m}}{\text{km}}, \qquad
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = 30\,\frac{\text{m}}{\text{km}}.
</md>
At that same instant the hiker is moving east at <m>3</m> km/h and north
at <m>4</m> km/h:
<md>
\frac{dx}{dt} = 3\,\frac{\text{km}}{\text{h}}, \qquad
\frac{dy}{dt} = 4\,\frac{\text{km}}{\text{h}}.
</md>
How quickly is the hiker's elevation increasing, in meters per hour?
</p>

<image width="50%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-hiker-velocity">
<diagram dimensions="(330,290)" margins="(8,8,8,8)">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-1.0,5.3,4.7)">
<line at="hv-diagonal" endpoints="((0,0),(3,4))" stroke="#555555" dash="6 4" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
<line at="hv-east" endpoints="((0,0),(3,0))" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<line at="hv-north" endpoints="((3,0),(3,4))" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<point at="hv-start" p="(0,0)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
<label anchor="(1.5,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-4)" color="#0072B2"><m>3\text{ km/h east}</m></label>
<label anchor="(3,2)" alignment="e" offset="(6,0)" color="#D55E00"><m>4\text{ km/h north}</m></label>
<label anchor="(1.5,2.0)" alignment="nw" offset="(-2,2)"><m>\text{actual direction}</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The hiker's velocity in the horizontal plane, drawn to scale: a blue arrow of 3 kilometers per hour to the east, followed by an orange arrow of 4 kilometers per hour to the north. A dashed arrow from the start to the tip of the orange arrow is the actual direction of motion.">
  <annotation ref="hv-east" text="The eastward component, 3 kilometers per hour."/>
  <annotation ref="hv-north" text="The northward component, 4 kilometers per hour."/>
  <annotation ref="hv-diagonal" text="The actual direction of motion, the sum of the two components."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
By the chain rule <xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/>,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dw}{dt} \amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
      + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \underbrace{(40)(3)}_{\text{east}}
      + \underbrace{(30)(4)}_{\text{north}}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 120 + 120 = 240\,\text{m/h}.</mrow>
</md>
The units work out: <m>\frac{\text{m}}{\text{km}}\cdot\frac{\text{km}}{\text{h}}
= \frac{\text{m}}{\text{h}}</m>. The hiker's elevation is increasing at
<m>240</m> meters per hour, and each component of the motion accounts for
<m>120</m> m/h of this increase.
</p>
<p>
<alert>Keep the two speeds distinct.</alert> The hiker's horizontal walking
speed is <m>\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5</m> km/h, whereas the vertical climbing
rate is <m>240</m> m/h. The question asks for the latter.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<!-- The espresso example and its practice problems: from the instructor's
     handout "Pulling an Espresso Shot" (Nov 2026). The statement shows the
     instructor's photo with its hints taken out (assets/espresso-shot.jpg:
     no incoming-stream label, and the box on the cup asks "C(t) = ?");
     the solution ends with the full picture (espresso-shot-summary.jpg). -->
<example xml:id="example-cr-espresso">
<title>Pulling an Espresso Shot</title>
<statement>
<p>
As an espresso shot pours, coffee solids dissolve into the water and the
cup fills. The strength of what is in the cup is its
<term>concentration</term>
<md>
C(m,V) = \frac{m}{V},
</md>
where <m>m</m> is the mass of dissolved coffee solids in the cup (in
grams) and <m>V</m> is the volume of liquid in the cup (in milliliters).
Both change during the shot, so <m>m = m(t)</m> and <m>V = V(t)</m>, with
<m>t</m> in seconds.
</p>

<image source="espresso-shot.jpg" width="70%">
<shortdescription>
An espresso shot pouring into a glass cup, labelled with m of t and V of t, and a box on the cup asking C of t equals what.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A photograph of an espresso shot pouring from a machine into a clear glass
cup. A label pointing at the cup reads <q><m>m(t)</m>, mass of dissolved
solids (g)</q>, a second reads <q><m>V(t)</m>, volume of liquid (mL)</q>,
and a box on the cup asks <q><m>C(t) = {}</m>?</q>.
</p>
</description>
</image>

<p>
At <m>t = 20</m> s,
<md>
<mrow>m = 4\,\text{g}, \quad V = 40\,\text{mL},</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dm}{dt} = 0.12\,\text{g/s}, \quad \frac{dV}{dt} = 1.6\,\text{mL/s}.</mrow>
</md>
At <m>t = 20</m> s, is the cup getting stronger or weaker, and how fast?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>Step 1: Dependence.</alert> <m>C</m> depends on <m>m</m> and
<m>V</m>, and each of those depends on <m>t</m>, as the tree diagram
below shows.
</p>

<image width="46%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-tree-espresso">
<diagram dimensions="(280,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-0.5,1.7,3.5)">
<line at="te-cm" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.2,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="te-cv" endpoints="((0,3),(1.2,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="te-mt" endpoints="((-1.2,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="te-vt" endpoints="((1.2,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="te-c" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="te-m" p="(-1.2,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="te-v" p="(1.2,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="te-t" p="(0,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="te-c-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n">
<m>C=\frac{m}{V}</m>
</label>
<label at="te-m-lab" anchor="(-1.3,1.5)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>m</m>
</label>
<label at="te-v-lab" anchor="(1.3,1.5)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>V</m>
</label>
<label at="te-t-lab" anchor="(0,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
<m>t</m>
</label>
<label at="te-cm-lab" anchor="(-0.72,2.4)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial C}{\partial m}</m>
</label>
<label at="te-cv-lab" anchor="(0.72,2.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial C}{\partial V}</m>
</label>
<label at="te-mt-lab" anchor="(-0.72,0.6)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dm}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="te-vt-lab" anchor="(0.72,0.6)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dV}{dt}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A diamond shaped tree diagram. At the top is the concentration C equals m over V. Two dashed blue branches descend from it, the left labelled partial C partial m leading to a node m, and the right labelled partial C partial V leading to a node V. From m and V two further branches, labelled d m d t and d V d t, descend and meet at the bottom node t.">
  <annotation ref="te-c" text="The concentration C at the top of the tree."/>
  <annotation ref="te-m" text="The intermediate variable m, the mass of dissolved coffee solids."/>
  <annotation ref="te-v" text="The intermediate variable V, the volume of liquid in the cup."/>
  <annotation ref="te-t" text="The independent variable t, time in seconds, at the bottom."/>
  <annotation ref="te-cm" text="The branch from C to m contributes the factor partial C partial m."/>
  <annotation ref="te-mt" text="The branch from m to t contributes the factor d m d t. Multiplying along this path gives the first term of the chain rule."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>

<p>
<alert>Step 2: Chain rule.</alert> There are two paths from <m>C</m> down
to <m>t</m>, so <xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/>, with <m>C</m> in place
of <m>w</m>, has two terms:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dC}{dt} \amp = \frac{\partial C}{\partial m}\frac{dm}{dt}
      + \frac{\partial C}{\partial V}\frac{dV}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{V}\frac{dm}{dt} - \frac{m}{V^2}\frac{dV}{dt}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>Step 3: Plug in.</alert> First,
<m>C = \frac{4}{40} = 0.10\,\text{g/mL}</m>, about <m>10\%</m>, a typical
espresso strength. Then
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dC}{dt} \amp = \frac{0.12}{40} - \frac{4}{40^2}\,(1.6)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0.003 - 0.004</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -0.001\,(\text{g/mL})/\text{s}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>Step 4: Interpret.</alert> The concentration is
<em>decreasing</em> by about <m>0.001</m> g/mL (<m>0.1</m> percentage
point) per second. Solids are still going into the cup, but the cup is
getting weaker.
</p>
<p>
<alert>Why?</alert> Write <m>m' = \frac{dm}{dt}</m> and
<m>V' = \frac{dV}{dt}</m>, and factor the derivative:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dC}{dt} \amp = \frac{1}{V}\bigl(m' - C\,V'\bigr)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{V'}{V}\left(\frac{m'}{V'} - C\right).</mrow>
</md>
The ratio <m>m'/V'</m> is the concentration of the stream <em>coming out
of the machine right now</em>. Since <m>V'/V \gt 0</m>, the cup gets
weaker exactly when the stream is weaker than the cup:
<md>
\frac{dC}{dt} \lt 0 \iff \frac{m'}{V'} \lt C.
</md>
Here the stream is <m>0.12/1.6 = 0.075\,\text{g/mL}</m> (<m>7.5\%</m>),
weaker than the <m>10\%</m> already in the cup, so adding it dilutes the
shot. This is why baristas cut a shot once the stream turns pale.
</p>

<image source="espresso-shot-summary.jpg" width="100%">
<shortdescription>
Summary of the solution: the espresso photo with the incoming stream labelled, the tree diagram, the chain rule, and its interpretation.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The espresso photograph, now with the stream leaving the machine labelled
<q>Incoming stream <m>\frac{m'(t)}{V'(t)}</m></q>. Beside it, a tree
diagram: <m>C(m,V)</m> at the top, arrows down to <m>m(t)</m> and
<m>V(t)</m>, and from each an arrow down to <m>t</m>. Below the tree, the
chain rule <m>\frac{dC}{dt} = \frac{\partial C}{\partial m}\frac{dm}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial C}{\partial V}\frac{dV}{dt}
= \frac{1}{V}\frac{dm}{dt} - \frac{m}{V^2}\frac{dV}{dt}</m>. Along the
bottom, the interpretation: the term <m>\frac{1}{V}\frac{dm}{dt}</m> adds
solids and makes the cup stronger, the term
<m>-\frac{m}{V^2}\frac{dV}{dt}</m> adds liquid and makes the cup weaker,
and <m>\frac{dC}{dt} \lt 0</m> exactly when
<m>\frac{m'(t)}{V'(t)} \lt C(t)</m>.
</p>
</description>
</image>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-cr-espresso-practice">
<title>Pulling an Espresso Shot, Continued</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Use the same data at <m>t = 20</m> s as in
<xref ref="example-cr-espresso"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-cr-espresso-constant">
<statement>
<p>
What flow rate <m>\frac{dV}{dt}</m> would make the strength of the cup
momentarily constant at <m>t = 20</m> s (with <m>m</m>, <m>V</m>, and
<m>\frac{dm}{dt}</m> unchanged)? Interpret your answer.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Set <m>\frac{dC}{dt} = 0</m>:
<md>
\frac{1}{V}\frac{dm}{dt} = \frac{m}{V^2}\frac{dV}{dt},
</md>
so
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dV}{dt} \amp = \frac{V}{m}\frac{dm}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{40}{4}\,(0.12) = 1.2\,\text{mL/s}.</mrow>
</md>
At <m>1.2</m> mL/s, the stream's concentration is
<m>0.12/1.2 = 0.10\,\text{g/mL}</m>, exactly the cup's concentration.
Adding liquid of the same strength leaves the strength unchanged. A
faster flow dilutes the cup, and a slower flow strengthens it.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-espresso-linear">
<statement>
<p>
Use a linear approximation to estimate the concentration at
<m>t = 22</m> s.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
A linear approximation replaces the graph of <m>C</m> near <m>t = 20</m>
by its tangent line there,
<md>
<mrow>L(t) \amp = C(20) + \left.\frac{dC}{dt}\right|_{t=20}(t - 20)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0.10 - 0.001\,(t - 20).</mrow>
</md>
Close to <m>t = 20</m> the curve and its tangent line are close, so
<m>C(22) \approx L(22)</m>. With <m>\Delta t = 2</m> s,
<md>
<mrow>C(22) \amp \approx C(20) + \left.\frac{dC}{dt}\right|_{t=20}\Delta t</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0.10 + (-0.001)(2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0.098\,\text{g/mL},</mrow>
</md>
about <m>9.8\%</m>.
</p>

<image width="85%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-espresso-linear">
<diagram dimensions="(440,280)" margins="(46,14,22,14)">
<!-- A sketch: only C(20) = 0.10 and C'(20) = -0.001 are known, so the
     curve is illustrative. The tangent line at t = 20 is exact. -->
<definition>Cs(t) = 0.10 - 0.001*(t - 20) + 0.00012*(t - 20)^2</definition>
<definition>L(t) = 0.10 - 0.001*(t - 20)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(16.6, 0.0948, 24.6, 0.1040)">
<group at="axes">
<line endpoints="((17, 0.0955), (24.4, 0.0955))" stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
<line endpoints="((17, 0.0955), (17, 0.1037))" stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
<label anchor="(24.4, 0.0955)" alignment="se" offset="(2,-2)"><m>t</m></label>
<label anchor="(17, 0.1037)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,0)"><m>C</m></label>
<label anchor="(18, 0.0955)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-3)"><m>18</m></label>
<label anchor="(20, 0.0955)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-3)"><m>20</m></label>
<label anchor="(22, 0.0955)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-3)"><m>22</m></label>
<label anchor="(17, 0.098)" alignment="w" offset="(-3,0)"><m>0.098</m></label>
<label anchor="(17, 0.100)" alignment="w" offset="(-3,0)"><m>0.100</m></label>
<label anchor="(17, 0.102)" alignment="w" offset="(-3,0)"><m>0.102</m></label>
<line endpoints="((18, 0.0955), (18, 0.09568))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<line endpoints="((20, 0.0955), (20, 0.09568))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<line endpoints="((22, 0.0955), (22, 0.09568))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<line endpoints="((17, 0.098), (17.12, 0.098))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<line endpoints="((17, 0.100), (17.12, 0.100))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
<line endpoints="((17, 0.102), (17.12, 0.102))" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
</group>
<group at="guides">
<line endpoints="((20, 0.0955), (20, 0.100))" stroke="gray" dash="4 4" thickness="1"/>
<line endpoints="((17, 0.100), (20, 0.100))" stroke="gray" dash="4 4" thickness="1"/>
<line endpoints="((22, 0.0955), (22, 0.098))" stroke="gray" dash="4 4" thickness="1"/>
<line endpoints="((17, 0.098), (22, 0.098))" stroke="gray" dash="4 4" thickness="1"/>
</group>
<graph at="curve" function="Cs" domain="(17.5, 23.6)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2.5"/>
<graph at="tangent" function="L" domain="(17.5, 23.6)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<point at="base" p="(20, 0.100)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
<group at="estimate">
<point p="(22, L(22))" fill="#D55E00" size="3.5"/>
<point p="(22, Cs(22))" fill="#0072B2" size="3.5"/>
<line endpoints="((22.3, L(22)), (22.3, Cs(22)))" stroke="black" thickness="3"/>
<label anchor="(22.3, 0.09824)" alignment="e" offset="(4,0)"><m>\text{error}</m></label>
</group>
<label at="curve-lab" anchor="(17.6, Cs(17.6))" alignment="ne" offset="(4,2)" color="#0072B2"><m>C(t)</m></label>
<label at="tangent-lab" anchor="(23.3, L(23.3))" alignment="sw" offset="(-2,-4)" color="#D55E00"><m>L(t)</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A sketch of the concentration C of t near t equals 20, with its tangent line L of t at t equals 20. The estimate for C of 22 is read off the tangent line.">
  <annotation ref="curve" text="The graph of C of t, drawn in blue. Only its value and slope at t equals 20 are known, so this curve is a sketch." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="tangent" text="The tangent line L of t equals 0.10 minus 0.001 times t minus 20, drawn in orange." sonify="yes"/>
  <annotation ref="base" text="The point t equals 20, C equals 0.10, where the curve and its tangent line touch."/>
  <annotation ref="estimate" text="At t equals 22 the tangent line is at 0.098, the estimate. The curve is a little away from it; the short vertical gap is the error of the linear approximation."/>
  <annotation ref="axes" text="The horizontal axis is time t in seconds, with 18, 20 and 22 marked. The vertical axis is the concentration C in grams per milliliter, with 0.098, 0.100 and 0.102 marked."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>

<p>
In the sketch above only <m>C(20)</m> and the slope there are known, so
the blue curve is drawn for illustration; the tangent line is exact. The
estimate is read off the tangent line at <m>t = 22</m>, and the short
vertical gap between line and curve there is the error of the
approximation, which shrinks as <m>t</m> gets closer to <m>20</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-espresso-paths">
<statement>
<p>
Explain, using the tree diagram, why the chain rule for
<m>\frac{dC}{dt}</m> has two terms, and what each term means physically.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>C</m> depends on <m>t</m> only <em>through</em> <m>m</m> and
<m>V</m>, and the tree has two paths from <m>C</m> down to <m>t</m>: one
through <m>m</m> and one through <m>V</m>. Each path contributes the
product of the derivatives along its edges, and the total rate is the
sum of the paths:
</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<alert>Path through <m>m</m>:</alert>
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial C}{\partial m}\frac{dm}{dt} \amp = \frac{1}{V}\frac{dm}{dt}
      = \frac{0.12}{40}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = +0.003\,(\text{g/mL})/\text{s}.</mrow>
</md>
New dissolved solids make the cup <em>stronger</em>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<alert>Path through <m>V</m>:</alert>
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial C}{\partial V}\frac{dV}{dt} \amp = -\frac{m}{V^2}\frac{dV}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -\frac{4}{1600}\,(1.6)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -0.004\,(\text{g/mL})/\text{s}.</mrow>
</md>
New liquid makes the cup <em>weaker</em>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>
The two effects compete. At <m>t = 20</m> s dilution wins, so
<m>\frac{dC}{dt} \lt 0</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</example>

<theorem xml:id="thm-chain-rule-three-intermediate">
<title>Chain Rule for Functions of Three Independent Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the three-variable function <m>w = f(x,y,z)</m>. If the
following conditions are satisfied:
</p>
<p>
<alert>1.</alert> <m>w = f(x,y,z)</m> has continuous partial derivatives
<m>f_x</m>, <m>f_y</m>, and <m>f_z</m>,
</p>
<p>
<alert>2.</alert> <m>x = x(t)</m>, <m>y = y(t)</m>, and <m>z = z(t)</m>
are differentiable functions of <m>t</m>,
</p>
<p>
then the function <m>w = f(x(t), y(t), z(t))</m> is a differentiable
function of <m>t</m> and
<md xml:id="eq-chain-rule-three" number="yes">
\frac{dw}{dt}
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\frac{dz}{dt}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Again the tree diagram of <xref ref="fig-cr-tree-three"/> provides an
easy way to remember the chain rule: now there are three paths from
<m>w</m> down to <m>t</m>, so the formula has three terms.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-tree-three">
<caption>
Tree diagram for <m>w = f(x(t), y(t), z(t))</m>. Each of the three
intermediate variables contributes one term to
<xref ref="eq-chain-rule-three"/>.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-tree-three">
<diagram dimensions="(320,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.1,-0.5,2.1,3.5)">
<line at="t3-wx" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.5,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-wy" endpoints="((0,3),(0,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-wz" endpoints="((0,3),(1.5,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-xt" endpoints="((-1.5,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-yt" endpoints="((0,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="t3-zt" endpoints="((1.5,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="t3-w" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="t3-x" p="(-1.5,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t3-y" p="(0,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t3-z" p="(1.5,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="t3-t" p="(0,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="t3-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n">
<m>w=f(x,y,z)</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-x-lab" anchor="(-1.6,1.5)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>x</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-y-lab" anchor="(0.12,1.58)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>y</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-z-lab" anchor="(1.6,1.5)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>z</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-t-lab" anchor="(0,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
<m>t</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-wx-lab" anchor="(-0.95,2.4)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-wy-lab" anchor="(-0.08,2.35)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-wz-lab" anchor="(0.95,2.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-xt-lab" anchor="(-0.95,0.6)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-yt-lab" anchor="(0.1,0.72)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="t3-zt-lab" anchor="(0.95,0.6)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dz}{dt}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A tree diagram with w equals f of x y z at the top, three intermediate nodes x, y and z in a row below it, and the single node t at the bottom. Dashed blue branches connect w to each of the three intermediate variables and each of those down to t, giving three complete paths.">
  <annotation ref="t3-w" text="The dependent variable w at the top."/>
  <annotation ref="t3-y" text="The middle intermediate variable y, on the vertical branch."/>
  <annotation ref="t3-t" text="The independent variable t at the bottom. Because three separate paths lead from w to t, the chain rule has three terms."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<!-- The Americano example and its follow-up: the espresso shot of
     example-cr-espresso with hot water added, for the three-variable
     chain rule (Nov 2026). -->
<example xml:id="example-cr-americano">
<title>Pulling an Americano</title>
<statement>
<p>
Some espresso machines make an Americano by adding hot water to the cup
while the shot is still pouring. The cup now holds the espresso of
<xref ref="example-cr-espresso"/> together with the added water, so its
concentration is
<md>
C(m,V,W) = \frac{m}{V + W},
</md>
where <m>m</m> is the mass of dissolved coffee solids in the cup (in
grams), <m>V</m> is the volume of espresso (in milliliters), and
<m>W</m> is the volume of added hot water (in milliliters). All three
change during the shot, so <m>m = m(t)</m>, <m>V = V(t)</m> and
<m>W = W(t)</m>, with <m>t</m> in seconds. At <m>t = 20</m> s,
<md>
<mrow>m = 4\,\text{g}, \amp \quad \frac{dm}{dt} = 0.12\,\text{g/s},</mrow>
<mrow>V = 40\,\text{mL}, \amp \quad \frac{dV}{dt} = 1.6\,\text{mL/s},</mrow>
<mrow>W = 40\,\text{mL}, \amp \quad \frac{dW}{dt} = 2\,\text{mL/s}.</mrow>
</md>
At <m>t = 20</m> s, is the cup getting stronger or weaker, and how fast?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>Step 1: Dependence.</alert> <m>C</m> depends on <m>m</m>,
<m>V</m> and <m>W</m>, and each of those depends on <m>t</m>, as the tree
diagram below shows.
</p>

<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-tree-americano">
<diagram dimensions="(320,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.1,-0.5,2.1,3.5)">
<line at="ta-cm" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.5,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ta-cv" endpoints="((0,3),(0,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ta-cw" endpoints="((0,3),(1.5,1.5))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ta-mt" endpoints="((-1.5,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ta-vt" endpoints="((0,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="ta-wt" endpoints="((1.5,1.5),(0,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="ta-c" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="ta-m" p="(-1.5,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ta-v" p="(0,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ta-w" p="(1.5,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ta-t" p="(0,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="ta-c-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n">
<m>C=\frac{m}{V+W}</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-m-lab" anchor="(-1.6,1.5)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>m</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-v-lab" anchor="(0.12,1.58)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>V</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-w-lab" anchor="(1.6,1.5)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>W</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-t-lab" anchor="(0,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
<m>t</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-cm-lab" anchor="(-0.95,2.4)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial C}{\partial m}</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-cv-lab" anchor="(-0.08,2.35)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial C}{\partial V}</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-cw-lab" anchor="(0.95,2.4)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial C}{\partial W}</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-mt-lab" anchor="(-0.95,0.6)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dm}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-vt-lab" anchor="(0.1,0.72)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dV}{dt}</m>
</label>
<label at="ta-wt-lab" anchor="(0.95,0.6)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dW}{dt}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A tree diagram with the concentration C equals m over V plus W at the top, three intermediate nodes m, V and W in a row below it, and the single node t at the bottom. Dashed blue branches connect C to each of the three intermediate variables and each of those down to t, giving three complete paths.">
  <annotation ref="ta-c" text="The concentration C at the top."/>
  <annotation ref="ta-m" text="The intermediate variable m, the mass of dissolved coffee solids."/>
  <annotation ref="ta-v" text="The intermediate variable V, the volume of espresso, on the vertical branch."/>
  <annotation ref="ta-w" text="The intermediate variable W, the volume of added hot water."/>
  <annotation ref="ta-t" text="The independent variable t, time in seconds, at the bottom. Because three separate paths lead from C to t, the chain rule has three terms."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>

<p>
<alert>Step 2: Chain rule.</alert> There are three paths from <m>C</m>
down to <m>t</m>, so <xref ref="eq-chain-rule-three"/>, with <m>C</m> in
place of <m>w</m>, has three terms:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dC}{dt} \amp = \frac{\partial C}{\partial m}\frac{dm}{dt}
      + \frac{\partial C}{\partial V}\frac{dV}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \frac{\partial C}{\partial W}\frac{dW}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{V+W}\frac{dm}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad - \frac{m}{(V+W)^2}\frac{dV}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad - \frac{m}{(V+W)^2}\frac{dW}{dt}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>Step 3: Plug in.</alert> First,
<m>C = \frac{4}{40 + 40} = 0.05\,\text{g/mL}</m>, about <m>5\%</m>, half
the strength of the straight shot. Then
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dC}{dt} \amp = \frac{0.12}{80} - \frac{4}{80^2}\,(1.6)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad - \frac{4}{80^2}\,(2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0.0015 - 0.001 - 0.00125</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -0.00075\,(\text{g/mL})/\text{s}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>Step 4: Interpret.</alert> The concentration is
<em>decreasing</em> by about <m>0.00075</m> g/mL (<m>0.075</m> percentage
point) per second. The solids path adds <m>+0.0015</m>, and the two
dilution paths take away <m>0.001</m> (espresso) and <m>0.00125</m>
(water), so dilution wins.
</p>
<p>
<alert>Why?</alert> As in <xref ref="example-cr-espresso"/>, write
<m>m' = \frac{dm}{dt}</m>, <m>V' = \frac{dV}{dt}</m>,
<m>W' = \frac{dW}{dt}</m>, and factor the derivative:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dC}{dt} \amp = \frac{m' - C\,(V' + W')}{V+W}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{V'+W'}{V+W}\left(\frac{m'}{V'+W'} - C\right).</mrow>
</md>
What enters the cup now is espresso and water together, and
<m>\frac{m'}{V'+W'}</m> is the concentration of that combined stream.
Here it is <m>0.12/3.6 \approx 0.033\,\text{g/mL}</m>
(<m>3.3\%</m>), weaker than the <m>5\%</m> already in the cup, so the cup
gets weaker. The espresso stream by itself, at <m>7.5\%</m>, is stronger
than the cup: it is the added water that tips the balance.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-cr-americano-practice">
<title>Pulling an Americano, Continued</title>
<introduction>
<p>
Use the same data at <m>t = 20</m> s as in
<xref ref="example-cr-americano"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-cr-americano-steady">
<statement>
<p>
At what rate <m>\frac{dW}{dt}</m> should the hot water flow to keep the
strength of the cup momentarily constant at <m>t = 20</m> s (with
everything else unchanged)? What happens if the water is turned off?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
By the factored form, <m>\frac{dC}{dt} = 0</m> exactly when
<m>m' = C\,(V' + W')</m>, that is,
<md>
<mrow>V' + W' \amp = \frac{m'}{C} = \frac{0.12}{0.05}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 2.4\,\text{mL/s},</mrow>
<mrow>W' \amp = 2.4 - 1.6</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0.8\,\text{mL/s}.</mrow>
</md>
At that rate the combined stream is <m>0.12/2.4 = 0.05\,\text{g/mL}</m>,
the cup's own strength. Faster water dilutes the cup and slower water
lets it strengthen. With the water off (<m>W' = 0</m>) only the
<m>7.5\%</m> espresso stream comes in, stronger than the cup, and
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dC}{dt} \amp = \frac{1}{80}\bigl(0.12 - 0.05\,(1.6)\bigr)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = +0.0005\,(\text{g/mL})/\text{s},</mrow>
</md>
so the cup gets stronger.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-americano-total-volume">
<statement>
<p>
In this example <m>\frac{\partial C}{\partial V} = \frac{\partial C}{\partial W}</m>.
Explain why, and use it to redo the computation with the chain rule for
two intermediate variables, <xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Both partial derivatives equal <m>-\frac{m}{(V+W)^2}</m> because
<m>C</m> depends on <m>V</m> and <m>W</m> only through the total volume
<m>U = V + W</m>: the cup cannot tell espresso from tap water. So
<m>C = \frac{m}{U}</m>, a function of the two intermediate variables
<m>m</m> and <m>U</m>, with
<md>
<mrow>U \amp = 40 + 40 = 80\,\text{mL},</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{dU}{dt} \amp = \frac{dV}{dt} + \frac{dW}{dt} = 3.6\,\text{mL/s}.</mrow>
</md>
Then <xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/> gives
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dC}{dt} \amp = \frac{1}{U}\frac{dm}{dt} - \frac{m}{U^2}\frac{dU}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{0.12}{80} - \frac{4}{6400}\,(3.6)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0.0015 - 0.00225</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -0.00075\,(\text{g/mL})/\text{s},</mrow>
</md>
the same answer: the <m>V</m> and <m>W</m> terms of the three-variable
rule combine into the single <m>U</m> term.
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cr-two-independent">
<title>Two Independent Variables</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-chain-rule-rs">
<title>Chain Rule for Two Independent and Two Intermediate Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two-variable function <m>w = f(x,y)</m>. If
<m>w = f(x,y)</m>, <m>x = x(r,s)</m>, and <m>y = y(r,s)</m> are
differentiable functions, then the partial derivatives of the function
<m>w(r,s) = f(x(r,s), y(r,s))</m> are given by
<md xml:id="eq-chain-rule-rs" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial w}{\partial r}
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial r}\\
\amp\quad + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial r},\\[0.5em]
\frac{\partial w}{\partial s}
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial s}\\
\amp\quad + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial s}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
The tree diagram now has two variables at the bottom, so we read it once
for <m>r</m> and once for <m>s</m>, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-cr-tree-rs"/>. In each case we follow only the branches
leading down to the variable we are differentiating with respect to.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-tree-rs">
<caption>
Two readings of the same tree for two independent variables. On the left,
the branches leading to <m>r</m> give
<m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial r}</m>; on the right, the branches leading
to <m>s</m> give <m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial s}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-tree-rs">
<diagram dimensions="(600,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.9,-0.9,7.9,3.6)">
<!-- left tree: highlight branches to r -->
<line at="rs-l-wx" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.2,1.6))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-wy" endpoints="((0,3),(1.2,1.6))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-xr" endpoints="((-1.2,1.6),(-0.8,0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-yr" endpoints="((1.2,1.6),(-0.8,0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-xs" endpoints="((-1.2,1.6),(0.8,0))" stroke="#CCCCCC"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="rs-l-ys" endpoints="((1.2,1.6),(0.8,0))" stroke="#CCCCCC"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="rs-l-w" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="rs-l-x" p="(-1.2,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="rs-l-y" p="(1.2,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="rs-l-r" p="(-0.8,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="rs-l-s" p="(0.8,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="rs-l-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n"><m>w=f(x,y)</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-x-lab" anchor="(-1.3,1.6)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-y-lab" anchor="(1.3,1.6)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-r-lab" anchor="(-0.8,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73"><m>r</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-s-lab" anchor="(0.8,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#999999"><m>s</m></label>
<label at="rs-l-eq" anchor="(0,-0.62)" alignment="c" color="#D55E00">
<m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial r}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial r}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial r}</m>
</label>
<!-- right tree: highlight branches to s -->
<line at="rs-r-wx" endpoints="((6,3),(4.8,1.6))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-wy" endpoints="((6,3),(7.2,1.6))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-xs" endpoints="((4.8,1.6),(6.8,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-ys" endpoints="((7.2,1.6),(6.8,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-xr" endpoints="((4.8,1.6),(5.2,0))" stroke="#CCCCCC"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="rs-r-yr" endpoints="((7.2,1.6),(5.2,0))" stroke="#CCCCCC"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="rs-r-w" p="(6,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="rs-r-x" p="(4.8,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="rs-r-y" p="(7.2,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="rs-r-r" p="(5.2,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="rs-r-s" p="(6.8,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="rs-r-w-lab" anchor="(6,3.1)" alignment="n"><m>w=f(x,y)</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-x-lab" anchor="(4.7,1.6)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-y-lab" anchor="(7.3,1.6)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-r-lab" anchor="(5.2,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#999999"><m>r</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-s-lab" anchor="(6.8,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#009E73"><m>s</m></label>
<label at="rs-r-eq" anchor="(6,-0.62)" alignment="c" color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial s}=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial s}+\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial s}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two copies of the same tree diagram side by side. Each has w equals f of x y at the top, the intermediate variables x and y below it, and the two independent variables r and s at the bottom. In the left copy the branches leading down to r are highlighted in orange and the branches to s are faint grey; in the right copy the branches leading to s are highlighted in blue instead. Under each tree is the corresponding chain rule formula.">
  <annotation ref="rs-l-xr" text="In the left tree, the highlighted branches are those running from w through x and through y down to r, giving the formula for the partial derivative of w with respect to r."/>
  <annotation ref="rs-r-xs" text="In the right tree, the highlighted branches run down to s instead, giving the formula for the partial derivative of w with respect to s."/>
  <annotation ref="rs-l-s" text="The faint grey branches are the ones not used for this particular derivative."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-cr-exp-sin">
<title>Two Independent Variables</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>z = f(x,y) = e^x \sin y</m>, where
<m>x = st^2</m> and <m>y = s^2 t</m>, and evaluate
<m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial s}</m> and
<m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial t}</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Here the independent variables are <m>s</m> and <m>t</m>. We have
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = e^x \sin y</m> and
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = e^x \cos y</m>, together with
<m>\frac{\partial x}{\partial s} = t^2</m>,
<m>\frac{\partial y}{\partial s} = 2st</m>,
<m>\frac{\partial x}{\partial t} = 2st</m>, and
<m>\frac{\partial y}{\partial t} = s^2</m>. By
<xref ref="eq-chain-rule-rs"/>,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial z}{\partial s}
      \amp= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial s}
      + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial s}
      = (e^x \sin y)(t^2) + (e^x \cos y)(2st)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= t^2 e^{st^2}\sin(s^2 t) + 2st\,e^{st^2}\cos(s^2 t),</mrow>
</md>
and
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial z}{\partial t}
      \amp= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial t}
      + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial t}
      = (e^x \sin y)(2st) + (e^x \cos y)(s^2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= 2st\,e^{st^2}\sin(s^2 t) + s^2 e^{st^2}\cos(s^2 t).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cr-applications">
<title>More Examples</title>

<example xml:id="example-cr-xy-circle">
<title>Differentiating Along a Circle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>w = f(x,y) = xy</m>, where <m>x = \cos t</m> and
<m>y = \sin t</m>. Evaluate
<m>\left.\dfrac{dw}{dt}\right|_{t=\pi/2}</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>Method 1: using the chain rule.</alert> By
<xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/>,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dw}{dt} \amp= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
      + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
      = (y)(-\sin t) + (x)(\cos t)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= (\sin t)(-\sin t) + (\cos t)(\cos t)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= \cos^2 t - \sin^2 t = \cos(2t),</mrow>
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\left.\frac{dw}{dt}\right|_{t=\pi/2} = \cos(\pi) = -1.
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>Method 2: without using the chain rule.</alert> We can substitute
first and then differentiate:
<md>
<mrow>w = f(x,y) = xy \amp \;\Rightarrow\; w(t) = \cos t \sin t
= \frac{1}{2}\sin(2t)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \;\Rightarrow\; \frac{dw}{dt} = \cos(2t),</mrow>
</md>
so again
<md>
\left.\frac{dw}{dt}\right|_{t=\pi/2} = \cos(\pi) = -1.
</md>
Note that the two results agree with each other.
</p>
<p>
Geometrically, the constraint <m>x = \cos t</m>, <m>y = \sin t</m>
confines the point <m>(x,y)</m> to the unit circle
<m>x^2+y^2 = 1</m>, so the composition traces a curve on the saddle
<m>z = xy</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-cr-saddle"/>. The derivative
<m>\frac{dw}{dt}</m> is the rate at which the height of that curve
changes.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-saddle">
<caption>
The curve traced on the surface <m>z = xy</m> as <m>(x,y)</m> runs around
the unit circle <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>. Marked are the points at <m>t = 0</m>,
<m>t = \pi/4</m>, and <m>t = \pi/2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="100%">
<description>
<p>
A saddle shaped surface, the graph of z equals x y, shaded blue where it
dips below the x y plane and gold where it rises above it. A closed green
curve is drawn on the surface, lying above the unit circle in the x y
plane, which is drawn in dark purple. Three points on the curve are marked
in magenta, red and black, corresponding to t equals zero, t equals pi
over four, and t equals pi over two. A legend above the axes names the
surface, the circle, the curve and the three marked points.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\fontfamily{phv}\fontsize{10}{12.5}\selectfont]
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=8.5cm,
  view={-35}{24},
  xmin=-1.45, xmax=1.45, ymin=-1.45, ymax=1.45, zmin=-0.95, zmax=0.95,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-1,0,1}, ytick={-1,0,1}, ztick={-0.5,0,0.5},
  clip=false,
  % blue where the surface falls below the xy-plane, gold where it rises above
  % it, so the two rising and two falling quarters of the trip read apart
  colormap={saddle}{rgb255(0cm)=(70,148,190)
                    rgb255(1cm)=(238,240,242)
                    rgb255(2cm)=(228,158,72)},
  point meta min=-0.9, point meta max=0.9,
  legend cell align=left,
  legend columns=2,
  legend style={
    at={(0.5,1.03)}, anchor=south,
    draw=gray!50, fill=white, fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1,
    inner sep=5pt, row sep=2pt,
    /tikz/every even column/.append style={column sep=10pt},
    font=\fontfamily{phv}\fontsize{10}{12.5}\selectfont,
  },
]
% the saddle z = xy, drawn over the disc that the unit circle sits inside so
% that it stays within the axis box instead of running off the top and bottom
% shader=flat, not interp: an interpolated shader emits a PDF smooth shading,
% which the pdf-to-svg step flattens to a low-resolution bitmap and leaves the
% rim of the disc visibly pixelated. Flat cells stay vector, and at this mesh
% density they read as a continuous gradient anyway.
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat corner, point meta=z,
          fill opacity=0.8, draw=mapped color, draw opacity=0.8, line width=0.1pt,
          z buffer=sort, forget plot,
          domain=0:1.32, y domain=0:360, samples=17, samples y=97]
  ({x*cos(y)},{x*sin(y)},{x^2*cos(y)*sin(y)});
% the unit circle in the xy-plane
\addplot3[very thick, violet!75!black, forget plot, domain=0:360, samples=64]
  ({cos(x)},{sin(x)},{0});
% the curve on the surface: (cos t, sin t, cos t sin t)
\addplot3[ultra thick, green!45!black, forget plot, domain=0:360, samples=80]
  ({cos(x)},{sin(x)},{cos(x)*sin(x)});
% marked points, ringed in white so they read against either colour
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=magenta, forget plot,
          mark options={fill=magenta, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}]
  coordinates {(1,0,0)};
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=red!70!black, forget plot,
          mark options={fill=red!70!black, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}]
  coordinates {(0.7071,0.7071,0.5)};
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=black, forget plot,
          mark options={fill=black, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}]
  coordinates {(0,1,0)};
% the legend: the three objects on the left, the three marked points on the
% right, in the order they are met as t runs from 0 to pi/2
\addlegendimage{area legend, draw=black!25, shading=axis,
                left color=blue!30, right color=orange!45}
\addlegendentry{the surface $z=xy$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=magenta,
                mark options={fill=magenta, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}}
\addlegendentry{$t=0:\;(1,0,0)$}
\addlegendimage{very thick, violet!75!black}
\addlegendentry{the circle $x^2+y^2=1$}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=red!70!black,
                mark options={fill=red!70!black, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}}
\addlegendentry{$t=\frac{\pi}{4}:\;\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt2},\frac{1}{\sqrt2},\frac12\right)$}
\addlegendimage{ultra thick, green!45!black}
\addlegendentry{the traced curve}
\addlegendimage{only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2, color=black,
                mark options={fill=black, draw=white, line width=0.5pt}}
\addlegendentry{$t=\frac{\pi}{2}:\;(0,1,0)$}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-cr-saddle-interactive"/> sets that picture moving. As
<m>t</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <m>2\pi</m> the point travels once round
the circle while the curve above it climbs and falls twice, and the
readout carries both the height <m>w</m> and the rate
<m>\frac{dw}{dt} = \cos 2t</m> the chain rule predicts. At
<m>t = \pi/2</m> that rate reads <m>-1</m>, the answer computed above.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-saddle-interactive">
<caption>
<xref ref="fig-cr-saddle"/>, set moving. Send the point round the circle
with the slider, or
jump to <m>t = 0</m>, <m>t = \pi/4</m> and <m>t = \pi/2</m>: the legend
gives the height <m>w = \tfrac12\sin 2t</m> of the curve and the rate
<m>\frac{dw}{dt} = \cos 2t</m> at which that height is changing, which is
the number the chain rule computes.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-cr-saddle"
             iframe="saddle-circle-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="3:2">
<instructions>
<p>
The saddle is <m>z = xy</m>, the mauve ring below it is the unit circle
<m>x = \cos t</m>, <m>y = \sin t</m>, and the green curve is the path
traced on the saddle as <m>t</m> increases. The white point marks the
current <m>t</m>; the arrow on it points along the curve and grows with
<m>\left|\frac{dw}{dt}\right|</m>, turning amber where the height is
rising and red where it is falling. The three coloured dots are the
marked points <m>t=0</m>, <m>t=\pi/4</m> and <m>t=\pi/2</m>. Drag the
picture to turn it.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-cr-second">
<title>A Second Application of the Chain Rule</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>w = f(x,y) = xy + y^2</m>, where
<m>x = \cos t</m> and <m>y = 1 + 2\sin t</m>. Evaluate
<m>\left.\dfrac{dw}{dt}\right|_{t=\pi}</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
By <xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/>, with
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = y</m> and
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = x + 2y</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{dw}{dt} \amp= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
      + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
      = (y)(-\sin t) + (x+2y)(2\cos t)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= (1+2\sin t)(-\sin t)
      + \bigl(\cos t + 2(1+2\sin t)\bigr)(2\cos t)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= -\sin t - 2\sin^2 t + 2\cos^2 t + 4\cos t
      + 8 \sin t \cos t.</mrow>
</md>
At <m>t = \pi</m> we have <m>\sin \pi = 0</m> and
<m>\cos \pi = -1</m>, so the surviving terms are
<m>2\cos^2 \pi = 2</m> and <m>4\cos \pi = -4</m>, giving
<md>
\left.\frac{dw}{dt}\right|_{t=\pi} = 2 - 4 = -2.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-cr-ant">
<title>The Temperature Experienced by a Moving Ant</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that the temperature on a metal plate is given by the function
<m>T</m> with
<md>
T(x,y) = 100 - (x^2 + 4y^2),
</md>
where the temperature is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and <m>x</m> and
<m>y</m> are each measured in feet. Now suppose that an ant is walking on
the metal plate in such a way that it walks in a straight line from the
point <m>(1,4)</m> to the point <m>(5,6)</m>.
<ol marker="a.">
<li>
<p>
Find parametric equations <m>(x(t), y(t))</m> for the ant's coordinates
as it walks the line from <m>(1,4)</m> to <m>(5,6)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What can you say about <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m> and <m>\frac{dy}{dt}</m> for
every value of <m>t</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Determine the instantaneous rate of change in temperature with respect to
<m>t</m> that the ant is experiencing at the moment it is halfway from
<m>(1,4)</m> to <m>(5,6)</m>, using your parametric equations for
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Include units on your answer.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>a.</alert> The slope of the line is
<md>
\text{slope} = \frac{6-4}{5-1} = \frac{1}{2},
</md>
so the line is <m>y - 4 = \frac12 (x-1)</m>. Choosing
<m>x - 1 = t</m>, that is <m>x = t+1</m>, gives
<md xml:id="eq-cr-ant-param" number="yes">
\begin{gathered}
x(t) = t+1,\\
y(t) = \frac{t}{2} + 4,\\
0 \le t \le 4.
\end{gathered}
</md>
At <m>t = 0</m> the ant is at <m>(1,4)</m> and at <m>t = 4</m> it is at
<m>(5,6)</m>, as required.
</p>
<p>
<alert>b.</alert> Differentiating <xref ref="eq-cr-ant-param"/>,
<md>
\frac{dx}{dt} = 1 \qquad \text{and} \qquad \frac{dy}{dt} = \frac{1}{2}
</md>
for every value of <m>t</m>. Both are constant, which reflects the fact
that the ant walks along a straight line at a steady rate.
</p>
<p>
<alert>c.</alert> The tree diagram for this composition is the one in
<xref ref="fig-cr-tree-two"/>, with <m>T</m> in place of <m>w</m>. By
<xref ref="eq-chain-rule-two"/>,
<md>
\frac{dT}{dt}
= \frac{\partial T}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial T}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
= (-2x)(1) + (-8y)\left(\frac12\right)
= -2x - 4y.
</md>
The halfway point is
<md>
\left(\frac{5+1}{2},\; \frac{6+4}{2}\right) = (3,5),
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\left.\frac{dT}{dt}\right|_{(3,5)} = (-2)(3) - (4)(5) = -26
</md>
degrees Fahrenheit per unit time.
</p>
<p>
Alternatively, <m>\frac{dT}{dt}</m> can be written in terms of <m>t</m>:
<md>
\frac{dT}{dt} = -2(t+1) - 4\left(\frac{t}{2}+4\right) = -4t - 18.
</md>
Also notice that at <m>t = 2</m> the ant is at <m>(3,5)</m>, and hence
<md>
\left.\frac{dT}{dt}\right|_{t=2} = (-4)(2) - 18 = -26
</md>
degrees Fahrenheit per unit time, in agreement with the first computation.
The temperature is falling as
the ant walks, which makes sense: it is moving away from the origin,
where <m>T</m> is largest.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-cr-identity">
<title>Proving an Identity with the Chain Rule</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that <m>w = f(xz, yz)</m>, where <m>f</m> is a differentiable
function. Show that
<md>
x\frac{\partial w}{\partial x} + y\frac{\partial w}{\partial y}
= z\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Let us name the intermediate variables
<md>
u = xz \qquad \text{and} \qquad v = yz,
</md>
so that <m>w = f(u,v)</m> with <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> each depending on
<m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>. The tree diagram is shown in
<xref ref="fig-cr-tree-identity"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-tree-identity">
<caption>
Tree diagram for <m>w = f(u,v)</m> with <m>u = xz</m> and
<m>v = yz</m>. Note that <m>z</m> appears under both intermediate
variables, which is why <m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}</m> collects two
terms while <m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial x}</m> and
<m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial y}</m> collect one each.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-tree-identity">
<diagram dimensions="(340,290)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.3,-0.5,2.3,3.4)">
<line at="ti-wu" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.2,1.6))" stroke="#8B1A4A"
      thickness="2"/>
<line at="ti-wv" endpoints="((0,3),(1.2,1.6))" stroke="#8B1A4A"
      thickness="2"/>
<line at="ti-ux" endpoints="((-1.2,1.6),(-1.9,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      thickness="2"/>
<line at="ti-uz" endpoints="((-1.2,1.6),(0,0))" stroke="#009E73"
      thickness="2"/>
<line at="ti-vz" endpoints="((1.2,1.6),(0,0))" stroke="#009E73"
      thickness="2"/>
<line at="ti-vy" endpoints="((1.2,1.6),(1.9,0))" stroke="#0072B2"
      thickness="2"/>
<point at="ti-w" p="(0,3)" fill="black"/>
<point at="ti-u" p="(-1.2,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ti-v" p="(1.2,1.6)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ti-x" p="(-1.9,0)" fill="black"/>
<point at="ti-z" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>
<point at="ti-y" p="(1.9,0)" fill="black"/>
<label at="ti-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.1)" alignment="n"><m>w</m></label>
<label at="ti-u-lab" anchor="(-1.28,1.68)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>u=xz</m>
</label>
<label at="ti-v-lab" anchor="(1.28,1.68)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>v=yz</m>
</label>
<label at="ti-x-lab" anchor="(-1.9,-0.12)" alignment="s"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="ti-z-lab" anchor="(0,-0.12)" alignment="s"><m>z</m></label>
<label at="ti-y-lab" anchor="(1.9,-0.12)" alignment="s"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="ti-wu-lab" anchor="(-0.75,2.42)" alignment="w" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial u}</m>
</label>
<label at="ti-wv-lab" anchor="(0.75,2.42)" alignment="e" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\frac{\partial w}{\partial v}</m>
</label>
<label at="ti-ux-lab" anchor="(-1.72,0.85)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>z</m>
</label>
<label at="ti-uz-lab" anchor="(-0.48,0.72)" alignment="e" color="#009E73">
<m>x</m>
</label>
<label at="ti-vz-lab" anchor="(0.48,0.72)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>y</m>
</label>
<label at="ti-vy-lab" anchor="(1.72,0.85)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>z</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A tree diagram with w at the top, the two intermediate variables u equals x z and v equals y z below it, and the three variables x, z and y along the bottom. The variable u connects down to both x and z, and v connects down to both z and y, so the middle node z is reached from both sides. The branches are labelled with the corresponding partial derivatives.">
  <annotation ref="ti-u" text="The intermediate variable u equals x z."/>
  <annotation ref="ti-v" text="The intermediate variable v equals y z."/>
  <annotation ref="ti-z" text="The variable z sits under both u and v, so two paths lead from w down to z and the derivative with respect to z has two terms."/>
  <annotation ref="ti-ux" text="The branch from u to x carries the factor z, since the partial derivative of x z with respect to x is z."/>
  <annotation ref="ti-uz" text="The branch from u to z carries the factor x, since the partial derivative of x z with respect to z is x."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Reading the branches that lead down to <m>z</m>, and using
<m>\frac{\partial u}{\partial z} = x</m> and
<m>\frac{\partial v}{\partial z} = y</m>, we get
<md xml:id="eq-cr-identity-z" number="yes">
\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}
= \frac{\partial w}{\partial u}\frac{\partial u}{\partial z}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial v}\frac{\partial v}{\partial z}
= \frac{\partial w}{\partial u}\,x
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial v}\,y.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Only one branch leads down to <m>x</m>, namely the one through <m>u</m>,
and <m>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x} = z</m>, so
<md xml:id="eq-cr-identity-x" number="yes">
\begin{gathered}
\frac{\partial w}{\partial x}
= \frac{\partial w}{\partial u}\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}\\
= \frac{\partial w}{\partial u}\,z,\\
\frac{\partial w}{\partial u} = \frac{1}{z}\frac{\partial w}{\partial x}.
\end{gathered}
</md>
Similarly, only one branch leads down to <m>y</m>, through <m>v</m>, and
<m>\frac{\partial v}{\partial y} = z</m>, so
<md xml:id="eq-cr-identity-y" number="yes">
\begin{gathered}
\frac{\partial w}{\partial y}
= \frac{\partial w}{\partial v}\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}\\
= \frac{\partial w}{\partial v}\,z,\\
\frac{\partial w}{\partial v} = \frac{1}{z}\frac{\partial w}{\partial y}.
\end{gathered}
</md>
</p>
<p>
Substituting <xref ref="eq-cr-identity-x"/> and
<xref ref="eq-cr-identity-y"/> into
<xref ref="eq-cr-identity-z"/> gives
<md>
\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}
= \frac{1}{z}\frac{\partial w}{\partial x}\,x
+ \frac{1}{z}\frac{\partial w}{\partial y}\,y,
</md>
and multiplying both sides by <m>z</m> yields
<md>
z\frac{\partial w}{\partial z}
= x\frac{\partial w}{\partial x} + y\frac{\partial w}{\partial y},
</md>
which is the identity we wanted to prove.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-cr-doppler-boat">
<title>A Doppler-Effect Activity</title>

<activity xml:id="activity-cr-doppler-boat">
<title>Meeting Waves from a Moving Boat</title>

<introduction>
<p>
When a boat travels against approaching waves, it meets crests more
frequently. When it travels with the waves, it meets them less frequently.
This activity uses the chain rule to explain both observations.
</p>

<p>
Three quantities must be kept distinct. The function
<m>x_b(t)</m> is the boat's <em>horizontal position</em>,
<m>x_c(t)</m> is the <em>horizontal position of one selected wave
crest</em>, and <m>w(t)</m> is the signed <em>vertical water height at
the boat</em>, measured from the mean water level. Thus
<m>x_b(t)</m> and <m>x_c(t)</m> are coordinates on the same position
axis, while <m>w(t)</m> points perpendicular to that axis.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-quantities">
<caption>
The meanings of <m>x_b(t)</m>, <m>x_c(t)</m>, and <m>w(t)</m>.
The first two are horizontal positions measured from the same origin;
the last is the vertical water height sampled at the boat's position.
</caption>
<image width="88%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-doppler-quantities">
<diagram dimensions="(480,250)" margins="14">
<definition>poswater(s) = 0.8*cos(pi*(s-6)/2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.8,-1.75,9.0,1.45)">
<line at="dop-pos-mean" endpoints="((0,0),(8.4,0))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<graph at="dop-pos-wave" function="poswater" domain="(0,8.4)"
       stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-pos-axis" endpoints="((0,-1.5),(8.5,-1.5))"
      stroke="black" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-pos-axis-lab" anchor="(8.55,-1.5)" alignment="e">
<m>s</m>
</label>
<point at="dop-pos-origin" p="(0,-1.5)" fill="black"/>
<label at="dop-pos-origin-lab" anchor="(-0.08,-1.55)" alignment="sw">
<m>0</m>
</label>

<line at="dop-pos-xb-span" endpoints="((0,-1.08),(3.5,-1.08))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-pos-xb-lab" anchor="(1.75,-1.02)" alignment="n"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>x_b(t)</m>
</label>
<line at="dop-pos-xc-span" endpoints="((0,-1.38),(6,-1.38))"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-pos-xc-lab" anchor="(4.75,-1.32)" alignment="n"
       color="#009E73">
<m>x_c(t)</m>
</label>

<line at="dop-pos-xb-guide" endpoints="((3.5,-1.5),(3.5,-0.5657))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="dop-pos-xc-guide" endpoints="((6,-1.5),(6,0.8))"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<point at="dop-pos-boat-sample" p="(3.5,-0.5657)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dop-pos-crest" p="(6,0.8)" fill="#009E73"/>

<line at="dop-pos-height" endpoints="((3.5,0),(3.5,-0.5657))"
      stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-pos-height-lab" anchor="(3.35,-0.28)" alignment="w"
       color="#8B1A4A">
<m>w(t)</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-pos-boat-text" anchor="(3.68,-0.58)" alignment="e"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>\text{boat}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-pos-crest-text" anchor="(6.1,0.95)" alignment="e"
       color="#009E73">
<m>\text{selected crest}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue sinusoidal water profile above a common horizontal position axis. An orange arrow from the origin to the boat marks x sub b of t, and a green arrow from the same origin to a selected crest marks x sub c of t. A dark red vertical arrow from the mean water level to the surface at the boat marks w of t.">
  <annotation ref="dop-pos-xb-span" text="The orange horizontal distance x sub b of t from the origin to the boat."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-pos-xc-span" text="The green horizontal distance x sub c of t from the same origin to the selected crest."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-pos-height" text="The vertical signed height w of t, measured from the mean water level to the water surface at the boat."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-pos-wave" text="A snapshot of the sinusoidal water surface."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-pos-boat-sample" text="The point on the water surface sampled by the boat."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-pos-crest" text="The selected wave crest."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Consider equally spaced water waves traveling to the right along a straight
line. Model the water-surface height at the boat by
<md xml:id="eq-cr-doppler-model" number="yes">
<mrow>w\amp=f(x_b,x_c)</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=A\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x_b-x_c)\right).
</mrow>
</md>
Here <m>A&gt;0</m> is the wave amplitude and <m>\lambda&gt;0</m> is
the distance between neighboring crests. The model treats the waves as
ideal sinusoids and neglects the boat's effect on the water.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-wave-model">
<caption>
A snapshot of the wave model. Neighboring crests are separated by one
wavelength <m>\lambda</m>; the selected crest is at <m>x_c(t)</m>, and
the boat samples the surface at <m>x_b(t)</m> as the pattern travels to
the right.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-doppler-wave-model">
<diagram dimensions="(500,235)" margins="14">
<definition>wavesnapshot(s) = 0.8*cos(pi*(s-6)/2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.25,-1.45,10.9,2.15)">
<line at="dop-wave-mean" endpoints="((0,0),(10.5,0))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<graph at="dop-wave-profile" function="wavesnapshot" domain="(0,10.5)"
       stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>

<line at="dop-wave-travel" endpoints="((7.7,1.75),(10.1,1.75))"
      stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-wave-travel-lab" anchor="(8.9,1.83)" alignment="n"
       color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\text{wave travel}</m>
</label>

<line at="dop-wave-lambda" endpoints="((2,1.25),(6,1.25))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<line at="dop-wave-lambda-left" endpoints="((2,0.8),(2,1.37))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="1"/>
<line at="dop-wave-lambda-right" endpoints="((6,0.8),(6,1.37))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="1"/>
<label at="dop-wave-lambda-lab" anchor="(4,1.32)" alignment="n"
       color="#E69F00">
<m>\lambda</m>
</label>

<polygon at="dop-wave-boat"
         points="((3.02,-0.42),(3.98,-0.42),(3.76,-0.72),(3.24,-0.72))"
         fill="#D55E00" fill-opacity="0.30" stroke="#D55E00"
         thickness="2"/>
<line at="dop-wave-boat-left" endpoints="((3.02,-0.42),(3.24,-0.72))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-wave-boat-mast" endpoints="((3.5,-0.42),(3.5,0.15))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<point at="dop-wave-boat-point" p="(3.5,-0.5657)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="dop-wave-boat-text" anchor="(3.72,-0.20)" alignment="e"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>\text{boat}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-wave-boat-lab" anchor="(3.5,-0.92)" alignment="s"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>x_b(t)</m>
</label>

<point at="dop-wave-selected-crest" p="(6,0.8)" fill="#009E73"/>
<line at="dop-wave-crest-guide" endpoints="((6,0),(6,0.8))"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<label at="dop-wave-crest-lab" anchor="(6.08,0.91)" alignment="e"
       color="#009E73">
<m>x_c(t)</m>
</label>
<line at="dop-wave-amplitude-guide" endpoints="((6,0.8),(6.68,0.8))"
      stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="dop-wave-amplitude" endpoints="((6.58,0),(6.58,0.8))"
      stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<label at="dop-wave-amplitude-lab" anchor="(6.76,0.40)" alignment="e"
       color="#8B1A4A">
<m>A</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue sinusoidal wave profile traveling to the right. An orange boat sits on the profile at x sub b of t. A selected green crest is marked at x sub c of t. A gold double arrow between two neighboring crests marks the wavelength lambda.">
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-profile" text="The idealized sinusoidal water surface at one instant."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-travel" text="A right-pointing arrow showing the direction in which the wave pattern travels."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-lambda" text="The horizontal separation lambda between two neighboring crests."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-boat" text="The boat at its horizontal position x sub b of t."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-boat-left" text="The thick left edge completes the outline of the boat's hull."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-selected-crest" text="The chosen crest at horizontal position x sub c of t."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-wave-amplitude" text="The vertical amplitude A from mean water level to crest height."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Suppose the boat travels with constant velocity <m>v</m>, while every
crest travels to the right with constant speed <m>k&gt;0</m>:
<md xml:id="eq-cr-doppler-positions" number="yes">
<mrow>x_b(t)\amp=vt,</mrow>
<mrow>x_c(t)\amp=kt.</mrow>
</md>
At <m>t=0</m>, the boat and the selected crest are both at the origin.
A positive <m>v</m> means the boat travels with the waves, and a
negative <m>v</m> means it travels against them.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-dependency-tree">
<caption>
The chain-rule dependencies for
<m>w=f(x_b(t),x_c(t))</m>. Multiplying along the two paths from
<m>w</m> to <m>t</m> and adding gives the total rate of change.
</caption>
<image width="52%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-doppler-dependency-tree">
<diagram dimensions="(330,300)" margins="14">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.35,-0.55,2.35,3.55)">
<line at="dop-tree-w-xb" endpoints="((0,3),(-1.45,1.5))"
      stroke="#D55E00" dash="5 4" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-tree-w-xc" endpoints="((0,3),(1.45,1.5))"
      stroke="#0072B2" dash="5 4" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-tree-xb-t" endpoints="((-1.45,1.5),(0,0))"
      stroke="#D55E00" dash="5 4" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-tree-xc-t" endpoints="((1.45,1.5),(0,0))"
      stroke="#0072B2" dash="5 4" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dop-tree-w" p="(0,3)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<point at="dop-tree-xb" p="(-1.45,1.5)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dop-tree-xc" p="(1.45,1.5)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="dop-tree-t" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>

<label at="dop-tree-w-lab" anchor="(0,3.13)" alignment="n"
       color="#8B1A4A">
<m>w=f(x_b,x_c)</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-xb-lab" anchor="(-1.58,1.5)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>x_b</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-xc-lab" anchor="(1.58,1.5)" alignment="e"
       color="#009E73">
<m>x_c</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-t-lab" anchor="(0,-0.13)" alignment="s">
<m>t</m>
</label>

<label at="dop-tree-w-xb-lab" anchor="(-0.95,2.48)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_b}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-w-xc-lab" anchor="(0.95,2.48)" alignment="e"
       color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_c}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-xb-t-lab" anchor="(-0.95,0.55)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>\frac{dx_b}{dt}=v</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-tree-xc-t-lab" anchor="(0.95,0.55)" alignment="e"
       color="#0072B2">
<m>\frac{dx_c}{dt}=k</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A diamond-shaped dependency tree. The water height w equals f of x sub b and x sub c at the top. Orange and green intermediate nodes x sub b and x sub c lie in the middle, and time t lies at the bottom. The branches carry the two partial derivatives and the velocities v and k.">
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-w-xb" text="The branch from w to the boat position carries partial f with respect to x sub b."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-xb-t" text="The branch from the boat position to time carries d x sub b over d t, equal to v."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-w-xc" text="The branch from w to the selected crest position carries partial f with respect to x sub c."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-xc-t" text="The branch from the selected crest position to time carries d x sub c over d t, equal to k."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-tree-t" text="Both paths end at the independent variable t."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-video">
<caption>
The moving-wave model, its chain-rule tree, the rate calculation, three
boat-speed comparisons, nonzero cancellation when the boat keeps pace
with the waves, and the encounter-period formula.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-cr-doppler"
       source="videos/chain_rule_doppler_effect_manim.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/chain_rule_doppler_effect_manim.jpg"
       width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-rate">
<statement>
<p>
Find the rate of change of <m>w</m> with respect to <m>t</m> using the
chain rule.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Let
<m>\theta=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(x_b-x_c)</m>. The two partial
derivatives are
<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_b}
\amp=-\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}\sin\theta,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_c}
\amp=\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}\sin\theta.
</mrow>
</md>
Evaluating at <m>(x_b(t),x_c(t))</m> and following the two paths in
<xref ref="fig-cr-doppler-dependency-tree"/> gives
<md xml:id="eq-cr-doppler-rate" number="yes">
<mrow>
\frac{dw}{dt}
\amp=f_{x_b}x_b'+f_{x_c}x_c'
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}\sin\theta\,(-v+k)
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp=\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}(k-v)\sin\theta.
</mrow>
</md>
Using <m>x_b-x_c=(v-k)t</m>, define
<m>\theta(t)=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(v-k)t</m>. Then
<md>
\frac{dw}{dt}=\frac{2\pi A}{\lambda}(k-v)\sin\theta(t).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-constant-height">
<statement>
<p>
How can the water-surface height at the boat stay at zero (the mean water
level) for all time, even though the waves are moving?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The boat must keep pace with the waves. If <m>v\ne k</m>, the distance
<m>x_b-x_c</m> keeps changing, so the height at the boat runs through
crests and troughs. If <m>v=k</m>, then <m>x_b-x_c</m> is constant, so
the height is constant, and it is zero exactly when the boat sits a
quarter wavelength from a crest, where the cosine in the model
vanishes. For example, choose
<md>
<mrow>x_b(t)\amp=kt-\frac{\lambda}{4},</mrow>
<mrow>x_c(t)\amp=kt.</mrow>
</md>
The phase is <m>-\pi/2</m>, so <m>w(t)=A\cos(-\pi/2)=0</m> for all
<m>t</m>, and since <m>\sin\theta=-1</m>,
<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_b}\frac{dx_b}{dt}
\amp=\frac{2\pi Ak}{\lambda},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x_c}\frac{dx_c}{dt}
\amp=-\frac{2\pi Ak}{\lambda}.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-nonzero-cancellation">
<caption>
Nonzero cancellation when <m>v=k</m>. With
<m>x_b(t)=kt-\lambda/4</m> and <m>x_c(t)=kt</m>, the boat remains on a
sloping, zero-height part of the wave. The boat-motion and crest-motion
terms have equal magnitude and opposite signs.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-doppler-nonzero-cancellation">
<diagram dimensions="(510,275)" margins="14">
<definition>cancelwave(s) = cos(pi*(s-4)/4)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.65,-2.25,9.25,2.15)">
<line at="dop-cancel-mean" endpoints="((0,0),(8.8,0))"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<graph at="dop-cancel-wave" function="cancelwave" domain="(0,8.8)"
       stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dop-cancel-boat-point" p="(2,0)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dop-cancel-crest-point" p="(4,1)" fill="#009E73"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-crest-guide" endpoints="((4,0),(4,1))"
      stroke="#009E73" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>

<polygon at="dop-cancel-boat"
         points="((1.55,0.12),(2.45,0.12),(2.27,-0.18),(1.73,-0.18))"
         fill="#D55E00" fill-opacity="0.30" stroke="#D55E00"
         thickness="2"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-boat-left" endpoints="((1.55,0.12),(1.73,-0.18))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-boat-mast" endpoints="((2,0.12),(2,0.52))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2"/>
<label at="dop-cancel-boat-lab" anchor="(1.82,0.33)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>x_b=kt-\frac{\lambda}{4}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-cancel-crest-lab" anchor="(4.1,1.1)" alignment="e"
       color="#009E73">
<m>x_c=kt</m>
</label>

<line at="dop-cancel-quarter" endpoints="((2,-1.72),(4,-1.72))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2" arrows="2"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-quarter-left" endpoints="((2,-1.55),(2,-1.86))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="1"/>
<line at="dop-cancel-quarter-right" endpoints="((4,-1.55),(4,-1.86))"
      stroke="#E69F00" thickness="1"/>
<label at="dop-cancel-quarter-lab" anchor="(3,-1.65)" alignment="n"
       color="#E69F00">
<m>\lambda/4</m>
</label>

<line at="dop-cancel-boat-term" endpoints="((1.72,0.22),(1.72,1.22))"
      stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-cancel-boat-term-lab" anchor="(1.55,1.38)" alignment="w"
       color="#D55E00">
<m>f_{x_b}x_b'=\frac{2\pi Ak}{\lambda}</m>
</label>
<line at="dop-cancel-crest-term" endpoints="((2.28,-0.22),(2.28,-1.22))"
      stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
<label at="dop-cancel-crest-term-lab" anchor="(4.65,-1.3)" alignment="c"
       color="#0072B2">
<m>f_{x_c}x_c'=-\frac{2\pi Ak}{\lambda}</m>
</label>
<label at="dop-cancel-sum-lab" anchor="(7.25,1.45)" alignment="c"
       color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\frac{dw}{dt}=0,\qquad w(t)=0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue sinusoidal snapshot with a green crest at x sub c equals k t and an orange boat a quarter wavelength to its left at x sub b equals k t minus lambda over four. At the boat, an orange upward arrow and a blue downward arrow represent equal nonzero chain-rule contributions that cancel.">
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-boat-point" text="The boat samples the mean-height crossing one quarter-wavelength behind the selected crest."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-boat-left" text="The thick left edge completes the outline of the boat's hull."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-boat-mast" text="The mast identifies the orange hull as the boat."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-crest-point" text="The selected crest at x sub c equals k t."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-quarter" text="The fixed horizontal separation lambda over four."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-boat-term" text="The positive boat-motion contribution, two pi A k over lambda."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-crest-term" text="The negative crest-motion contribution, negative two pi A k over lambda."/>
  <annotation ref="dop-cancel-sum-lab" text="The two contributions sum to zero, and the measured height remains zero."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The equal, nonzero terms cancel, so <m>dw/dt=0</m>. A zero derivative
at one isolated crest or trough need not mean <m>v=k</m>; matching
speeds is what keeps the height constant over time. Any other fixed
offset holds some other constant height between <m>-A</m> and
<m>A</m>. At a tracked crest, for instance, <m>w(t)=A</m>, but there
both chain-rule terms are <m>0</m> and nothing cancels.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cr-doppler-period">
<statement>
<p>
How does the boat's velocity affect the time between successive crests?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Substituting the position functions into the model gives
<md>
w(t)=A\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}(v-k)t\right).
</md>
For <m>v\ne k</m>, one complete cycle occurs when the cosine's argument
changes by <m>2\pi</m>. Therefore the time between successive crests is
<md xml:id="eq-cr-doppler-period" number="yes">
T=\frac{\lambda}{|k-v|},
\qquad v\ne k.
</md>
Equivalently, the encounter frequency is <m>|k-v|/\lambda</m>. When
<m>v=k</m>, the sampled height is constant, so it has no finite
fundamental period.
</p>

<p>
For example, suppose adjacent crests are <m>8</m> meters apart and
travel at <m>2</m> meters per second.
</p>

<table xml:id="table-cr-doppler-periods">
<title>
Time between crests for <m>\lambda=8\text{ m}</m> and
<m>k=2\text{ m/s}</m>
</title>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="major">
<cell>Boat's motion</cell>
<cell><m>v</m> (m/s)</cell>
<cell>Time between crests</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>Stationary</cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m> seconds</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>Traveling with the waves</cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m> seconds</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>Traveling against the waves</cell>
<cell><m>-1</m></cell>
<cell><m>8/3</m> seconds</cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

<figure xml:id="fig-cr-doppler-speed-comparison">
<caption>
Normalized height <m>w/A</m> over eight seconds for the three velocities
in <xref ref="table-cr-doppler-periods"/>. Traveling against the waves
produces three cycles, remaining stationary produces two, and traveling
with the waves produces one.
</caption>
<image width="94%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cr-doppler-speed-comparison">
<diagram dimensions="(520,360)" margins="14">
<definition>against(t) = 3 + 0.72*cos(3*pi*t/4)</definition>
<definition>stationary(t) = 0.72*cos(pi*t/2)</definition>
<definition>withwaves(t) = -3 + 0.72*cos(pi*t/4)</definition>
